Top-Level Throughput␀*Ñ
Top-Level Throughput¥Start here. Compare throughputs and occupancy side-by-side to classify the workload.
If SM Activity and Warp Occupancy are low while all throughputs are low, the GPU is not filled â€” check Launch & Dispatch for launch sizes and dispatch gaps.
If all throughputs are low but occupancy is moderate, the workload is latency-limited â€” look at SM Occupancy & Stalls for warp scheduling bottlenecks.
If one throughput is clearly dominant (above ~60%), the workload is throughput-limited in that unit â€” drill into the corresponding cache or memory group below.
If both compute and memory throughputs are high (above ~80%), you are near hardware limits â€” check for tail effects."w
Fpmsampling:tpc__ctas_active_realtime.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedBlock OccupancyJgb20y_TriageCompute_sampling"¾
ATriageCompute.tpc__warps_active.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedWarp OccupancyJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingi␀␀␀␀␀␀à?zÁTriageCompute.tpc__warps_active = CounterMetric(RerollUp(smsp__warps_active, RollupOp.sum, HwUnit.tpc), 'cumulative # of warps in flight') TriageCompute.tpc__warps_active:SetSustainedRate(96.0)"q
;pmsampling:fe__output_ops.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedFront End ThroughputJgb20y_TriageCompute_sampling"©
