FTriageCompute.tpc__average_registers_per_thread_shader_cs_realtime.pctAvg Registers/ThreadJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzáTriageCompute.tpc__threads_active_shader_cs_realtime = CounterMetric(tpc__warps_active_shader_cs_realtime * 32, 'cumulative # of active CS threads') TriageCompute.tpc__threads_active_shader_cs_realtime:SetSustainedRate(96*32)zTriageCompute.tpc__average_registers_per_thread_shader_cs_realtime = RatioMetric(tpc__sm_rf_registers_allocated_shader_cs_realtime, RollupOp.avg, TriageCompute.tpc__threads_active_shader_cs_realtime, RollupOp.avg, 256.0, 'average # of registers per thread, for CS warps')"
SM Block OccupancyùCheck whether the block-slot limit (24 CTAs per SM) is the occupancy limiter. If this is near 100% while warp occupancy is low, each block has too few warps â€” increase threads per block, or restructure the work decomposition to merge small blocks."{
Fpmsampling:tpc__ctas_active_realtime.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedAverage CTAs ActiveJgb20y_TriageCompute_sampling"¤
L1TEX Data OccupancyâShows how the 128KB per-SM on-chip SRAM is divided between shared memory and L1 data cache. If Shared Memory dominates and occupancy is low, the per-CTA shared-memory allocation is the binding occupancy limiter â€” check Per-CTA Resource Usage. If Tagged Cache is small, L1 hit rates may suffer â€” see L1 Data Cache & LSU for cache-efficiency diagnosis.␀"
Jpmsampling:tpc__l1tex_sram_bytes_mem_tagged_realtime.avg.per_cycle_elapsedTagged Cache MemoryJgb20y_TriageCompute_sampling"£
jpmsampling:tpc__l1tex_sram_bytes_mem_untagged_data_shared_allocated_compute_realtime.avg.per_cycle_elapsedShared Memory (Compute)Jgb20y_TriageCompute_sampling"Ô
SMEM Allocated‹Shows how much of the shared-memory partition is actually used by resident CTAs. A large Shared Unallocated region suggests the shared-memory configuration is oversized for actual usage â€” the driver-configured SMEM size may be reducible, freeing space for L1 cache.␀"ã
cTriageCompute.tpc__l1tex_sram_bytes_mem_untagged_data_shared_compute_realtime.sum.per_cycle_elapsedSMEM Bytes AllocatedJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzÇTriageCompute.tpc__l1tex_sram_bytes_mem_untagged_data_shared_compute_realtime = CounterMetric(tpc__l1tex_sram_bytes_mem_untagged_realtime, 'cumulative # of shared memory bytes available for compute')"Ë
oTriageCompute.tpc__l1tex_sram_bytes_mem_untagged_data_shared_unallocated_compute_realtime.sum.per_cycle_elapsedSMEM Bytes UnallocatedJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingz¡TriageCompute.tpc__l1tex_sram_bytes_mem_untagged_data_shared_unallocated_compute_realtime = CounterMetric(tpc__l1tex_sram_bytes_mem_untagged_realtime - tpc__l1tex_sram_bytes_mem_untagged_data_shared_allocated_compute_realtime, 'cumulative # of shared memory bytes unallocated for compute')"¢
Per-CTA Resource UsageÖCompare per-CTA resource consumption against hardware limits to identify the occupancy bottleneck. If Avg SMEM/CTA is close to SMEM Config, shared memory is the binding limit â€” reduce per-block shared allocation or use dynamic sizing. Cross-reference with SM Register Occupancy and SM Block Occupancy to confirm which resource limits first."¬
bTriageCompute.tpc__average_bytes_mem_untagged_data_shared_allocated_compute_per_cta_realtime.ratioAvg SMEM/CTAJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingz™TriageCompute.tpc__average_bytes_mem_untagged_data_shared_allocated_compute_per_cta_realtime = RatioMetric(tpc__l1tex_sram_bytes_mem_untagged_data_shared_allocated_compute_realtime, RollupOp.avg, tpc__ctas_active_realtime, RollupOp.avg, 100*2*1024, 'average shared memory per CTA')"å
 Warp Stall Reasons (All Samples)”Sampled distribution of reasons warps could not issue. The tallest stall category is the dominant bottleneck.
Long Scoreboard: warps waiting on memory (global/local loads) â€” improve data locality or increase occupancy to hide latency.
LG Throttle: load/store unit is full â€” reduce memory instruction density or improve coalescing.
Barrier: warps waiting at __syncthreads â€” reduce synchronization frequency or balance work across threads.
If no single stall dominates, the workload may be well-balanced or simply underutilized."`
