Warp Stall ScoreboardÉBreakdown of scoreboard-related warp stalls. Long Scoreboard stalls indicate warps waiting on long-latency operations (typically global memory loads). Short Scoreboard stalls indicate warps waiting on short-latency operations (shared memory, L1 cache).
If Long Scoreboard dominates, improve global memory access patterns or increase occupancy to hide memory latency.
If Short Scoreboard dominates, reduce shared-memory bank conflicts or L1 cache contention."Ž
Vpmsampling:smsp__warps_issue_stalled_long_scoreboard.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedLong Scoreboard StallsJgr10x_TriageCompute_sampling"
Wpmsampling:smsp__warps_issue_stalled_short_scoreboard.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedShort Scoreboard StallsJgr10x_TriageCompute_sampling"å
 Warp Stall Reasons (All Samples)”Sampled distribution of reasons warps could not issue. The tallest stall category is the dominant bottleneck.
Long Scoreboard: warps waiting on memory (global/local loads) â€” improve data locality or increase occupancy to hide latency.
LG Throttle: load/store unit is full â€” reduce memory instruction density or improve coalescing.
Barrier: warps waiting at __syncthreads â€” reduce synchronization frequency or balance work across threads.
If no single stall dominates, the workload may be well-balanced or simply underutilized."`
