Avg. Compute Warp Latency³Average number of cycles a warp is resident from creation to retirement. This is an absolute, run-comparable measure â€” a decrease confirms optimization progress.
If warp latency is high while IPC is low, warps spend most of their lifetime stalled â€” check Warp Stall Reasons for the dominant stall class.
If warp latency is high and IPC is also high, the warp simply has a large instruction count â€” focus on algorithmic reduction."Ñ
@TriageCompute.tpc__average_warp_latency_shader_cs_realtime.ratioAvg Compute Warp LatencyJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzÔTriageCompute.tpc__average_warp_latency_shader_cs_realtime = RatioMetric(tpc__warps_active_shader_cs_realtime, RollupOp.avg, tpc__warps_launched_shader_cs, RollupOp.avg, 96.0, 'average CS warp duration (cycles)')"‚
Avg. Block LatencyãAverage CTA lifetime in cycles. The gap between CTA Latency and CTA User Latency reveals overhead outside user kernel code (block setup, teardown, synchronization framework).
If CTA Latency is much larger than CTA User Latency, runtime/driver overhead per block is significant â€” try larger blocks to amortize this cost.
Compare with Avg. Compute Warp Latency: if block latency far exceeds warp latency, barriers or load imbalance across warps within the block are the likely cause.␀"‰
5TriageCompute.tpc__average_cta_latency_realtime.ratioCTA LatencyJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzÝTriageCompute.gr__ctas_launched_realtime = CounterMetric(gr__ctas_launched_queue_sync_realtime + gr__ctas_launched_queue_async_realtime, '# of CTAs launched') TriageCompute.gr__ctas_launched_realtime:SetSustainedRate(1.0)zÄTriageCompute.tpc__average_cta_latency_realtime = RatioMetric(tpc__ctas_active_realtime, RollupOp.sum, TriageCompute.gr__ctas_launched_realtime, RollupOp.sum, 1.0, 'average CTA duration (cycles)')"÷
:TriageCompute.tpc__average_cta_user_latency_realtime.ratioCTA User LatencyJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzÝTriageCompute.gr__ctas_launched_realtime = CounterMetric(gr__ctas_launched_queue_sync_realtime + gr__ctas_launched_queue_async_realtime, '# of CTAs launched') TriageCompute.gr__ctas_launched_realtime:SetSustainedRate(1.0)zÒTriageCompute.gr_total_ctas_launched_realtime = CounterMetric(TriageCompute.gr__ctas_launched_realtime + gr__workids_granted_as_ctas_realtime) TriageCompute.gr_total_ctas_launched_realtime:SetSustainedRate(1.0)zÓTriageCompute.tpc__average_cta_user_latency_realtime = RatioMetric(tpc__ctas_active_realtime, RollupOp.sum, TriageCompute.gr_total_ctas_launched_realtime, RollupOp.sum, 1.0, 'average user CTA duration (cycles)')"‰
"SM Instruction Latency Per Read OpÔAverage cycles a warp stalls waiting for each type of read to complete. Higher values indicate longer memory latency.
High Local/Global Read Latency points to cache misses or poor coalescing â€” check L1TEX Sector Hit Rate and Tag-Stage Bandwidth per Operation.
High Shared Read Latency usually means bank conflicts â€” check Shared Bank Conflict Ratios.
High Texture Read Latency suggests cache thrashing or large working sets â€” check L1TEX Cache Capacity per Warp."Þ
ETriageCompute.sm__average_inst_latency_pipe_lsu_mem_lg_cmd_read.ratioLocal Global Read LatencyJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzÛTriageCompute.sm__average_inst_latency_pipe_lsu_mem_lg_cmd_read = RatioMetric(sm__inst_pending_pipe_lsu_mem_lg_cmd_read_realtime, RollupOp.avg, sm__inst_executed_pipe_lsu_mem_lg_cmd_read_realtime, RollupOp.avg, 1.0, '')"è
ITriageCompute.sm__average_inst_latency_pipe_lsu_mem_shared_cmd_read.ratioShared Read LatencyJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzçTriageCompute.sm__average_inst_latency_pipe_lsu_mem_shared_cmd_read = RatioMetric(sm__inst_pending_pipe_lsu_mem_shared_cmd_read_realtime, RollupOp.avg, sm__inst_executed_pipe_lsu_mem_shared_cmd_read_realtime, RollupOp.avg, 1.0, '')"½
>TriageCompute.sm__average_inst_latency_pipe_tex_cmd_read.ratioTexture Read LatencyJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzÆTriageCompute.sm__average_inst_latency_pipe_tex_cmd_read = RatioMetric(sm__inst_pending_pipe_tex_cmd_read_realtime, RollupOp.avg, sm__inst_executed_pipe_tex_cmd_read_realtime, RollupOp.avg, 1.0, '')*à

SM Occupancy & StallsOnly investigate occupancy if SM Issue Throughput (in SM Compute Pipelines) is low. Occupancy is a means, not an end. The goal is hiding latency.
Fewer than 4 warps/SM is a starvation condition: launch bigger grids or increase block count.
Above ~16 warps/SM, additional occupancy rarely helps hide math-pipe latency.
If Warps Inactive (SM Active) is large, an occupancy limiter is preventing more warps from being scheduled - check the resource rows below (registers, shared memory, block count) to find which limit is binding.␀"¼
ATriageCompute.tpc__warps_active.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedWarps ActiveJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingi␀␀␀␀␀␀à?zÁTriageCompute.tpc__warps_active = CounterMetric(RerollUp(smsp__warps_active, RollupOp.sum, HwUnit.tpc), 'cumulative # of warps in flight') TriageCompute.tpc__warps_active:SetSustainedRate(96.0)"ò
LTriageCompute.sm__warps_inactive_sm_active.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedWarps Inactive (SM Active)Jgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzLTriageCompute.max_warps_per_sm = ConversionFactorMetric(48.0, DimUnit.warps)z™TriageCompute.sm__warps_inactive_sm_active = CounterMetric(sm__cycles_active * TriageCompute.max_warps_per_sm - RerollUp(smsp__warps_active, RollupOp.sum, HwUnit.sm), 'cumulative # of idle warp slots on active SMs') TriageCompute.sm__warps_inactive_sm_active:SetSustainedRate(48.0)´
L1 Data Cache & LSU␀"Ï
L1TEX LSU Throughput½Compare the LSUs two stages to classify the bottleneck.
If LSU Data-Stage > LSU Writeback, the bottleneck is in address processing â€” likely poor coalescing (global/local/surface) or bank conflicts (shared). Check per-space breakdown below and Shared Memory Bank Conflicts.
If LSU Writeback > LSU Data-Stage, wide uniform loads (64/128-bit) or high miss traffic is saturating the writeback path â€” check L1TEX Tag-Stage Bandwidth per Hit Miss."|
Lpmsampling:l1tex__data_pipe_lsu_wavefronts.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedLSU Data-StageJgb20y_TriageCompute_sampling"w
Hpmsampling:l1tex__lsu_writeback_active.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedLSU WritebackJgb20y_TriageCompute_sampling"—	
%L1TEX LSU Throughput per Memory Space¡Identify which memory space drives high LSU throughput.
High LGDS wavefronts indicate heavy local/global/DMA/surface traffic â€” break this down further in SM Memory Bandwidth per Space.
High Shared Misc indicates shuffles, redux, match, or S2R operations saturating the pipe.
High Surface wavefronts may indicate surface load/store overhead â€” consider switching to global loads where image filtering is not needed."†
