Avg. Compute Warp Latency³Average number of cycles a warp is resident from creation to retirement. This is an absolute, run-comparable measure â€” a decrease confirms optimization progress.
If warp latency is high while IPC is low, warps spend most of their lifetime stalled â€” check Warp Stall Reasons for the dominant stall class.
If warp latency is high and IPC is also high, the warp simply has a large instruction count â€” focus on algorithmic reduction."Ñ
@TriageCompute.tpc__average_warp_latency_shader_cs_realtime.ratioAvg Compute Warp LatencyJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzÔTriageCompute.tpc__average_warp_latency_shader_cs_realtime = RatioMetric(tpc__warps_active_shader_cs_realtime, RollupOp.avg, tpc__warps_launched_shader_cs, RollupOp.avg, 96.0, 'average CS warp duration (cycles)')"‚
Avg. Block LatencyãAverage CTA lifetime in cycles. The gap between CTA Latency and CTA User Latency reveals overhead outside user kernel code (block setup, teardown, synchronization framework).
If CTA Latency is much larger than CTA User Latency, runtime/driver overhead per block is significant â€” try larger blocks to amortize this cost.
Compare with Avg. Compute Warp Latency: if block latency far exceeds warp latency, barriers or load imbalance across warps within the block are the likely cause.␀"‰
